WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.220
- I am such a believer
in handwritten letters.

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.260
Pilot Pen is actually
our season long partner

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.480
because I love handwritten
letters so much.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.250
Our world is more digital than ever

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.020
which makes physical
letters more important

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.700
and special than ever.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.850
This next story proves
just that meet Amy and Dana

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:18.120
they met at summer camp, but lost touch

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.530
and they reconnected on Facebook.

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:21.330
But it wasn't until Dana's son had cancer

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:24.000
that Amy wrote her a
handwritten letter, and it led

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.430
to more letters and their
friendship was renewed

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:28.920
and also sparked a project
that connected hundreds

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:29.753
of other people.

00:00:29.753 --> 00:00:30.930
But we're gonna get to that later.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:33.003
Please welcome Dana and Amy, everybody.

00:00:34.112 --> 00:00:34.945
- Thank you.

00:00:34.945 --> 00:00:35.778
- So welcome to the show.

00:00:35.778 --> 00:00:37.170
- Thank you so much for having us here.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:38.130
- Yeah. You look beautiful.
- Thanks.

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:39.330
- I saw y'all in the lobby on the way in

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:40.277
and I was like, God, they look beautiful.

00:00:40.277 --> 00:00:41.640
I love the colors.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:42.810
So you were camp counselors?

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:43.643
Is that how y'all met?

00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:44.476
- That's how we met.

00:00:44.476 --> 00:00:46.350
We were Camp Olympia at Trinity, Texas

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:47.183
and we spent -

00:00:47.183 --> 00:00:48.016
- Trinity, Texas?

00:00:48.016 --> 00:00:49.500
- Yeah, we probably
spent two weeks together.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.053
And you know how you just make
a connection with somebody?

00:00:51.053 --> 00:00:51.886
- Yeah. Right away.

00:00:51.886 --> 00:00:52.990
- We both have huge personalities.

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:54.840
And then so it was like bang.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.190
We had a great time and then -

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.076
- I would love y'all.

00:00:58.076 --> 00:01:00.060
- Then we spent, we
walked away and we spent

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.310
the next 30 years doing
life totally apart.

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.380
And I always had her name kind of kicking

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:06.390
around in my head and
wondered what happened to her.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:07.950
I get on Facebook just like everybody else

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.950
I was like, boom, there's Dana.
And so, I friend request her

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:13.620
I found out right away, she
has five kids, four girls

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:16.050
and the youngest Parker, her only son.

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:18.510
And he has, you know, I
found out he had cancer.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:19.770
And so we messaged back and forth.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:21.630
I was like, I'm praying for
you, thinking about you.

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:23.310
And then when he relapsed, it was

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:25.320
like a bolt from God out of the sky.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:26.153
It was like, you know what?

00:01:26.153 --> 00:01:28.050
I'm a writer. I'm gonna
start writing him letters.

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:29.190
So the whole thing kicked off

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:31.650
when I started to write
Dana and Parker letters

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:34.410
at the Ronald McDonald House in Memphis.

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:35.400
- Oh wow.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.140
What was your experience like

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:38.850
just hearing from her outta nowhere?

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:40.530
- Well, first of all,
I was kind of surprised

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:43.380
because we had not seen her,
you know, besides Facebook here

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:44.213
and there.

00:01:44.213 --> 00:01:45.677
In so many years.

00:01:45.677 --> 00:01:50.400
But then I was so touched because we got,

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.520
Parker and I got a lot
of support and gifts

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:54.675
and things like that.

00:01:54.675 --> 00:01:58.560
But the difference with Amy
is that she kept writing.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.374
She kept writing week after week.

00:02:01.374 --> 00:02:03.750
And Parker was like, who is this woman?

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:06.360
And I'm like, someone
dad and I worked with at

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:07.620
at a camp years ago.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:10.200
And he's like, how cool
though that she thinks enough

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.210
of us to write us every week.

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.610
- Yeah
- So she continued that.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:18.180
And Parker fought a very brave battle.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:21.690
He never complained.

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:26.340
And our worst day happened
in January of 2015.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:30.930
And so we went home and there
was a letter in the mail

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:33.960
and the next week a letter,
and the next week a letter.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:35.700
Amy continued to write me.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.854
So finally I wrote her back.

00:02:37.854 --> 00:02:41.070
And it was just such a great
thing. We became pen pals.

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:44.992
I was able to share my
grief in a safe space.

00:02:44.992 --> 00:02:49.260
And it wasn't just myself
that started looking forward

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:53.070
to her letters, my family
would say, did it come yet?

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:56.400
You know, every week it, it
just made such a difference.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.350
She still writes me every single week.

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:00.450
- Oh my gosh. I love it.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:01.930
Oh, try not to cry.

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:05.130
Okay. Wait, Amy, so you
had an epiphany from this,

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:05.970
so what was it?

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:07.710
- Well, this free space was created

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:09.500
between the two of us where
I did a lot of sharing too.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:11.940
And it just impacted me profoundly.

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:13.050
And I was like, you know what?

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:15.120
If I can have this one
random girl in Louisiana

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:17.700
this one Facebook friend
just blow up my life,

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.920
what else is out there?

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.650
So I was like I'm gonna write
all the 580 of these people.

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:24.750
So I don't know that I thought
I was really gonna do it.

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:26.700
- Wait 580, what people,
where'd you get that number?

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:27.533
- Facebook friends.

00:03:27.533 --> 00:03:29.550
- Oh, that's how many
friends you have on Facebook?

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:31.140
- That's what I had.
- Oh my God.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:32.820
Okay. I'm like, I don't, I'm not on.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:33.653
So I don't know.

00:03:33.653 --> 00:03:34.860
But I'm just like, that's a lot.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:35.730
- Yeah, no, it was a lot.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:36.563
So I put all the names in a box.

00:03:36.563 --> 00:03:38.250
- It's a lot of pen and paper mam.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:39.360
- I started pulling those names out

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.820
and it just blew up my
life because you know

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:43.740
I wrote to a girl who helped
me when my grandmother died

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:45.660
I wrote to my seventh grade band friend.

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:47.220
I wrote to a guy I dated in college.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.280
I wrote to a girl I danced
with at a wedding, you know?

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:51.540
And then all these people
that just were names

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:53.910
on a list, I was like, you
know what it really is?

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:58.530
I'm connected to this treasure
full of amazing people.

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:00.180
And there's just so much hope in that.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:03.060
- Yeah. And connecting with
people and those relationships

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:04.410
that's really what life is about.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.506
The older we get, we realize
it's that simple right?

00:04:06.506 --> 00:04:07.440
- Right. Exactly.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:08.940
- Yeah. And that connection is so cool.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.400
And so the letter is turned
into even more writing.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:12.810
So tell us about the book.

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:14.430
Right. Well, I was about three quarters

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:15.840
through writing up 600 letters.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:16.673
And I was like, you know what?

00:04:16.673 --> 00:04:19.620
We got a special story here
with so much hope in it.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:20.700
I was like, I'm gonna write a book.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.250
And so the book is called Dear Dana

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:24.270
that time I went crazy

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:27.270
and wrote all 580 of my
Facebook friends a letter.

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:28.830
The longest subtitle in history.

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.870
- That's the most amazing title.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:32.610
- And so it takes those two narratives.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:33.900
So it takes Dana and I's story

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:35.520
and a lot of Dana's words are in it.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:37.050
You know, our pen paling at the beginning

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:40.560
and then it runs the Facebook
letter story through that.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.440
And we went on a book tour
this summer, you know, going

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.759
on a book tour with your best
friend, 17 different cities.

00:04:45.759 --> 00:04:48.300
And one of the best things
was people showed up over

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.910
and over again, Facebook
friends, people of that 580

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:53.700
with their letter in
pristine, you know, condition.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:55.140
They're like, like they were a rockstar

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:56.190
cause they had this letter.

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:57.510
And they kept them in special places.

00:04:57.510 --> 00:05:00.030
And it's just another
illustration how something

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:01.955
like a deliberate act, like a
handwritten letter can really

00:05:01.955 --> 00:05:04.260
it really can change someone's life.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:07.380
- Absolutely. Yeah. Well,
we shared this story

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:08.640
with Pilot Makers of FriXion

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:10.980
the go-to erasable pen of
the Kelly Clarkson Show.

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:13.620
And they love celebrating
those who create fearlessly

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:16.170
and of handwriting 580 letters.

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:18.570
Isn't that, I don't know what it is.

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:20.910
So Pilot wanted to do
something to honor Parker.

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:23.310
So they're making a $5,000 donation to

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:25.810
St. Jude Children's Research
Hospital in his name.

00:05:26.879 --> 00:05:28.336
- Thank you.
- Yeah.

00:05:28.336 --> 00:05:31.172
- Thank you Pilot. That's amazing.

00:05:31.172 --> 00:05:34.680
- It's a hard thing I can't
even imagine going through.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:36.750
But I do love that
something beautiful came

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:38.863
out of it and something
positive that is connected

00:05:38.863 --> 00:05:40.801
to Parker, you know?

00:05:40.801 --> 00:05:41.634
- Absolutely.

00:05:41.634 --> 00:05:43.253
- So that will go on
forever, which is beautiful.

